While a Mutex is like a single key to a single-occupancy restroom, a Semaphore is more like a bowl of physical passes for a shared study room that can hold a specific number of people (say, 3 people).

If a pass is available, you take one and go in. If the bowl is empty, you have to wait outside until someone leaves and puts their pass back in the bowl.

In an RTOS, semaphores are primarily used for two things:

Managing a finite pool of identical resources (Counting Semaphore).

Signaling between tasks to synchronize events (Binary Semaphore).

Type,Initial Count,Common Purpose
Binary Semaphore,Max range is 0 to 1,"Signaling: One task (or an Interrupt) tells another task, ""Hey, an event happened, wake up and process it!"""
Counting Semaphore,Max range is 0 to N,"Resource Management: Tracking a limited number of items, like keeping track of how many unread data packets are waiting in a buffer."

How this execution flows:
When the system starts, vProcessingTask hits xSemaphoreTake. Because the binary semaphore starts at 0, this high-priority task immediately blocks and goes to sleep. It uses 0% CPU cycles right now.

vTriggerTask runs, waits for 2 seconds, and prints its message.

vTriggerTask calls xSemaphoreGive(), incrementing the semaphore count to 1.

The RTOS instantly notices that the high-priority vProcessingTask was waiting for this exact key. It pauses the trigger task and wakes up the processing task to complete its job.

Once done, vProcessingTask loops back, tries to Take the semaphore again (which is now back to 0), and goes right back to sleep until the next 2-second trigger.

The Crucial Difference: Mutex vs. Semaphore
It is easy to mistake a Binary Semaphore for a Mutex because both have a maximum count of 1. However, their underlying behavior and intent are fundamentally different:

Ownership: A Mutex has ownership. Whichever task takes a Mutex must be the one to give it back. A Semaphore has no owner—Task A can "Take" it, and Task B (or an Interrupt) can "Give" it to unlock Task A.

Priority Inversion Protection: Mutexes have a mechanism called Priority Inheritance (if a low-priority task holds the lock, its priority is temporarily boosted if a high-priority task needs it). Semaphores do not have priority inheritance, which makes semaphores dangerous to use for strict resource locking, but perfect for structural signaling.

#include <Arduino.h>

// Handle for our Binary Semaphore
SemaphoreHandle_t xBinarySemaphore;

// Task that handles heavy processing
void vProcessingTask(void *pvParameters) {
  while(1) {
    // Wait indefinitely until the semaphore is "given" by the trigger task
    if (xSemaphoreTake(xBinarySemaphore, portMAX_DELAY) == pdTRUE) {
      
      // This code runs ONLY when signaled
      Serial.println("Processing Task: Signal received! Performing heavy math...");
      vTaskDelay(pdMS_TO_TICKS(100)); // Simulate heavy work
      Serial.println("Processing Task: Work done. Going back to sleep.");
      
    }
  }
}

// Task that simulates an external trigger (like a sensor threshold or button press)
void vTriggerTask(void *pvParameters) {
  while(1) {
    vTaskDelay(pdMS_TO_TICKS(2000)); // Wait 2 seconds
    
    Serial.println("\nTrigger Task: An event occurred! Signaling Processing Task...");
    
    // "Give" the semaphore to wake up the processing task
    xSemaphoreGive(xBinarySemaphore);
  }
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  
  // Create a binary semaphore (starts in an empty/0 state)
  xBinarySemaphore = xSemaphoreCreateBinary();
  
  if (xBinarySemaphore != NULL) {
    // We give the processing task a higher priority so it responds instantly when signaled
    xTaskCreate(vProcessingTask, "Processor", 2048, NULL, 2, NULL);
    xTaskCreate(vTriggerTask, "Trigger", 2048, NULL, 1, NULL);
  }
}

void loop() {}

